<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gebel Adda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jebel Adda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a> was a fortified hilltop town on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, near the modern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. Occupied from the Meroitic period into Ottoman times, it is generally identified with Daw, the late medieval seat of the Nubian kingdom of Dotawo. Excavated in the 1960s before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> was filled, it now lies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gebel-adda.jpg" alt="Gebel Adda" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mileham, Geoffrey S; Randall-MacIver, David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gebel_Adda.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a> was a table mountain with steep slopes on every side, rising from flat ground on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> between the first and second cataracts, five kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> and 20 kilometres north of the present border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. The town on its crest could be reached only by a steep, narrow path with stairs, which led through a northern suburb to a massive gate reinforced in the 14th century. Its houses of clay brick, roofed with Nubian vaults, stood close together along narrow, winding streets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On the slope below, a small rock temple dedicated to Amun-Re and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a> was cut in the reign of the pharaoh Haremheb (c. 1319–1292 BCE); early Christians later plastered over its reliefs and painted it as a church. The hilltop was settled at least from the late Meroitic period, probably from the 2nd century CE, and between the 2nd and 4th centuries it was an important provincial centre together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a>. In the late Christian period it was, with Faras and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasr-ibrim">Qasr Ibrim</a>, one of the largest fortified towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, and much of it was rebuilt in the 13th century.</p><p>In 1276 forces sent by Sultan Baibars I fought at Gebel Adda on their way to Dongola, and in 1364 the Makurian rulers, with Egyptian support, won a victory there over insurgents. The fortress is thought to have been their seat from 1365 to around 1500, and a letter from Gebel Adda dated 1484 names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joel-of-dotawo">Joel of Dotawo</a>. The town stayed inhabited through the Ottoman period until the later 18th century, when its people probably moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballana">Ballana</a> on the other bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ugo Monneret de Villard described the fortress and its tombs after his work of 1932–1933, Mustafa el-Amir of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> began systematic excavation in 1959, and Nicholas B. Millet led four campaigns for the American Research Center between 1962 and 1965, before the site was flooded. Much of the excavated material is kept in the Royal Ontario Museum and remains unpublished. Parts of the rock temple's church were cut out and rebuilt near the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gebel Adda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Arab sources indicate that around 1365 Old Dongola was sacked amid a civil war between royal rivals and their Arab allies, and the surviving court withdrew north to <b>Gebel Adda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Dongola was abandoned around 1365 amid Mamluk interventions and Arab raids, the surviving court moved north to Daw, generally identified with <b>Gebel Adda</b> in Lower Nubia, and the reduced kingdom is known by this name.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dotawo">Kingdom of Dotawo</a></li></ul></section>
